function:
Evidence and examples support the progressive differentiation and integration of the thesis by helping the intended audience understand, accept, evaluate, or appreciate particular elaborative points.

Their functions may include:

• strengthening the credibility of an elaborative point;

• making an abstract idea concrete;

• illustrating a relationship, mechanism, distinction, condition, implication, or pattern;

• helping the audience recognize how an elaborative point operates in a particular case;

• providing grounds for accepting or evaluating an elaborative point;

• increasing the audience’s confidence in the integrated message developed throughout the paragraph.

Evidence and examples do not perform the principal intellectual work of the paragraph.

That work belongs to elaboration.

discovery_instruction:
Compass teaches:

• what evidence and examples are;

• why they are subordinate to elaboration;

• how evidence differs from elaboration;

• how examples make an idea concrete without replacing thought;

• why evidence and examples require interpretation;

• how the intended audience determines whether support is needed;

• how to judge relevance, adequacy, direction, proportionality, and integration.

The central discovery question is:

“What would a naïve reader need in order to understand, accept, evaluate, or appreciate this elaborative point?”

Compass compares the learner’s material with the structural requirements and invites the learner to determine what supporting work is genuinely needed.

Compass never instructs the learner simply to add more evidence.

Compass never supplies evidence, examples, or interpretations that the learner should construct or obtain.
